{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And she saw him, that he was good.</b> Because Moses was born circumcised at six months. His mother examined him — whether the signs of his hair and his nails were complete — and she knew of him that he was viable, as we learned in Yevamot. Therefore <b>she hid him three months</b>, until the end of nine months, which is the time of delivery for most women. And when they came to examine her at the end of nine, she said to them: he was stillborn, and I cast him into the river. This is according to the plain sense. Another interpretation: <b>that he was good</b> — that he was beautiful, as in 'כי טובות הנה'. Another interpretation: <b>that he was good</b> — he was born circumcised. And since it is written of him 'כי טוב' because he was born circumcised, the people took to saying 'הודו לה׳ כי טוב' over the circumcision." "<b>And she hid him three months.</b> How so? As our Rabbis of blessed memory said: on the seventh of Adar Moses was born, and she hid him twenty-three days in Adar, and Nisan, and Iyar, and six in Sivan — the three months were completed, and then she placed him at the edge of the river. And on the very day he was destined to receive the Torah, the river carried him. <b>And she hid him three months.</b> Even though they said 'today the savior of Israel is born,' even so the decree 'lest he multiply' was not annulled, and they were casting every male into the river."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The word '(מהול)' appears in parentheses in the source; it reads as a gloss on why he was 'good,' i.e., born circumcised."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:55:50.448774345Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}